title: Imputed Righteousness – Clothed in the Righteousness of Christ
description: God imputes righteousness without works to him that believeth: Christ was made sin for us, that we might be made the righteousness of God in Him.
output: lessons/doctrine/topic-salvation-imputed-righteousness.html
highlight: imput; counted; reckon

# Built from the author's teachings under "Imputed righteousness" in studies/data/topics/salvation.json,
# with two related teachings (6501, 5865). The slide text is the author's own sentences, trimmed.

=== The doctrine
# from 1714, 5373
--- Imputed Righteousness [title]
overline: Doctrine · Salvation
heading: Imputed Righteousness
sub: Jesus took on our sin and paid the price for all of it. By believing this, we take on His righteousness.
refs: Romans 4:5; 2 Corinthians 5:21
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the study. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

# from 1714, 5373, 17764
--- It is an exchange
kicker: The doctrine
big: 2 Corinthians 5:21 | mark: that we might be made the righteousness of God in him
lead: Jesus took on our sin and paid the price for all of it. He was the propitiation. The ransom. The sacrifice.
p: By believing this, we take on His righteousness. His blood washes us clean. It is an exchange. That is the testament.
note: The truth of imputed righteousness of God being given the believer is found throughout scripture.

=== Counted for righteousness
# from 11431, 16679, 7073
--- Abraham believed God
kicker: Genesis 15 · Romans 4
scripture: Genesis 15:6
p: Not by the law. Not by circumcision, for Abraham was saved decades before God gave circumcision as an ordinance. But by believing God.
p: Abraham believed God’s promise to him, by faith, without knowing the exact means by which He would make good on the promise, **only that He would**. And it was counted for righteousness.
refs: Romans 4:3; Romans 4:20-25

# from 10778, 11431
--- Not of debt, but of grace
scripture: Romans 4:4-5 | mark: him that justifieth the ungodly
p: Paul quotes this and argues that if it were of works, that means God would be indebted to us. To pay us for our works. That we were owed.
callout: Grace is an unmerited and unearned gift or favor. Debt is deserved. Grace is undeserved.
p: If it were his willingness to obey God, then Abraham would have the glory. But all the glory is God’s. He did it all. We just have to trust Him as Abraham did.

# from 17810, 10781
--- Blessed is the man
kicker: Psalm 32 · Romans 4
scripture: Romans 4:6-8
lead: The truth is that, in Christ, whether it was in David’s day, or Paul’s or our own, God forgives our past transgressions, covers our current sin, and will not hold our future sins and iniquity against us.
p: The Lord will not impute sin, our ledger is clean, our transgressions forgiven. This is a promise of God.
refs: Psalm 32:1-2; Hebrews 10:17

=== The ledger cleared
# from 17812
--- Transgression, sin and iniquity
lead: While it isn’t entirely wrong to take the words transgression, sin and iniquity, and use them interchangeably, they do carry subtle differences in their meaning.
panel: Transgression | The wilful rebellion against the truth of God and His commands.
panel: Sin | The transgression of the Law; it also carries with it the idea of missing the mark, falling short. [[1 John 3:4]] [[Romans 3:23]]
panel: Iniquity | From the Hebrew word, *Avon*, meaning to twist, bend, or distort. The perverted and crooked nature of fallen man.

# from 17812
--- Imputed on our ledger
scripture: Isaiah 53:5-6
p: He suffered these things for our iniquity and transgressions, that we may be purged. That His righteousness be imputed on our ledger, even as His blood blots away our sins.
callout: On the cross, Jesus carried away our rebellion, covered our failures with His blood, and cleared the ledger of our crooked nature by imputing His own perfect righteousness to us. Past, present and future.

# from 5373, 1714
--- The propitiation for the whole world
xr: Romans 3:25 | Christ the propitiation, through faith in His blood
xr: 1 John 2:2 | For the sins of the whole world
xr: 2 Corinthians 5:19 | Trespasses not imputed
xr: Philippians 3:9 | The righteousness which is of God by faith
xr: Isaiah 53:11 | The righteous servant bears their iniquities
xr: Galatians 2:16 | Justified by faith, not by the law
p: By believing this, we take on His righteousness. His blood washes us clean. We are clothed in White of His perfection, our sins forgotten.

# from 10793, 7073
--- While we were yet sinners
kicker: Romans 5
scripture: Romans 5:8-9; Romans 5:19
p: When we were righteous? No, as sinners. Did we earn it through works, the keeping of the law? No, that would be debt, it is the imputed righteousness from Christ that justifies us in faith. It is the law of grace. We don’t deserve it.

=== Their own righteousness
# from 17764
--- Is imputation a lie?
panel-warn: The error | There are false religions that deny the imputation of righteousness by faith in Christ. They say that imputation is not taught in scripture.
panel-warn: The Council of Trent | Canon 11 on Justification anathematized the idea that people are justified “by the sole imputation of the justice of Christ, or by the sole remission of sins.”
panel: The answer | This is a lie. Let God be true, and every man a liar. All who come to the saving knowledge of Christ are made pure, in Him. Righteous, in Him. [[Romans 3:4]] [[Titus 3:5-7]]

# from 17764, 6217
--- Going about to establish their own
big: Romans 10:3-4 | mark: have not submitted themselves unto the righteousness of God
p: Our righteousness is as a filthy rag, our works could never justify us before a holy and perfect God.
p: When believers are faced with those full of zeal for the Lord, but going about to establish their own righteousness rather than the imputed righteousness of Jesus Christ, we sigh in exasperation and ask, “are your hearts still hardened?”

# from 4790
--- Counted as dung
kicker: Philippians 3
scripture: Philippians 3:8-9 | mark: not having mine own righteousness
p: If birth, circumcision and zeal for the law saved anyone, it would be Paul, but he counts all his own “righteousness” as dung, so that he may gain Christ.
p: Despite our having the imputed righteousness of Jesus Christ, we should strive for sanctification. Obeying the law, not for our own righteousness but for the love of God.

# from 4959
--- Exceeding the scribes and Pharisees
big: Matthew 5:20
p: Unlike the claimed righteousness of the scribes and Pharisees, who taught loopholes and exceptions to God’s law and accused Jesus of seeking to destroy it, we must have the righteousness of Christ to enter into the Kingdom of Heaven, exceeding those hypocrites’ false idea of righteousness.

# from 5430, 5865
--- The wedding garment
kicker: Matthew 22
scripture: Matthew 22:11-13
lit: His own filthy rags | | In his own filthy rags, his own righteousness, representing those who believe they can enter the Kingdom by their own works.
rev: The garment given | | The spotless white garment given to believers by the lamb of God. The imputed righteousness of Jesus Christ which covers all our sin.
note: We have been invited to the wedding party. Everything is ready. He’s provided us with wedding clothes.

=== Pictures and promises
# from 6501, 17572
--- Covered by His handiwork
fig: Coats of skins | Genesis 3:21 | Shedding of blood for the remission of sin. God gives them this gift, freely. Of His own handiwork, not ours.
fig: No spot in thee | Song of Solomon 4:7 | Just as we who are saved and sanctified by faith in Christ are pure when the Lord looks upon us. Clothed in righteousness. No sin imputed upon our new man.
refs: Colossians 1:21-22

# from 14957, 14933
--- Two tales of our lives
big: Hebrews 10:17
p: As a Christian, we have two tales of our lives. The one, details our sin and the other remembers it no more.
p: In the Christian life, we are not just our sin and failings. The sin of our flesh and the trespasses we may do are not imputed against us. God remembers them no more.
callout: If God sees our sin as far from us as far as east is from west, we should strive to be as gracious and merciful to our brethren. [[Psalm 103:12]]

# from 12974, 6095
--- Not for thy righteousness
kicker: Deuteronomy 9 · Romans 11
scripture: Deuteronomy 9:5
p: We are not given the kingdom over those who refused to believe because we are better than they are, but because we submitted ourselves to receive the imputed righteousness of Christ. There but for God’s grace go I.
p: In matters of righteousness we are all in need of a physician, Christ Jesus. [[Romans 11:18-20]]

# from 17810
--- Now is the day of salvation
big: 2 Corinthians 6:2
p: There is a time, it is today. There is a day, and it is the present.
p: If you haven’t accepted the truth that we are sinners in need of salvation, and that it is offered as a free gift to all who believe, through the grace of Almighty God, now is the time.

=== Summary
# from 1714, 10778, 17810, 17812, 17764, 5430, 14957
--- Summary
point: An exchange | Jesus took on our sin and paid the price for all of it. By believing this, we take on His righteousness.
point: Counted, not earned | Not by the law. Not by circumcision. But by believing God. Debt is deserved. Grace is undeserved.
point: Sin not imputed | God forgives our past transgressions, covers our current sin, and will not hold our future sins and iniquity against us.
point: Not our own | Not by our own righteousness but by submitting to the righteousness that is God’s.
point: The garment given | The imputed righteousness of Jesus Christ which covers all our sin.
point: Remembered no more | The one tale details our sin and the other remembers it no more.

